A new strategy is in order.  There is a reason you meet such a powerful
critter as a pony early in the game.  It's almost always better to turn a
powerful enemy into an ally.

If you don't mind being spoiled (the previous posts probably already did
that)..















The litchen corpses you gather in the first few levels are valuable treats
for herbavores like ponies.  Ponies themselves are pretty tough monsters in
the first few levels, but they quickly become horses and then warhorses.  A
tame warhorse is pretty much the toughest customer within the first 8
levels.  They should be able to take out just about anything, including
gaurds or vampires/ettins zombies that inhabit the lowest levels of the
mines.  It's essential to keep a warhorse well-fed.  So collect all of the
fruit and veggies that you can along the way.  If you see the message "your
warhorse seems confused" or "you feel worried about your horse" then toss
him a treat quickly.  If you don't want a pet horse at all, toss him
something edible like a food ration.  He won't eat it, but he'll intepret
your gesture as an act of goodwill and will leave you alone.

Pet management is one of the key skills in moving from a beginning nethacker
to a midlevel player.  Try letting your pet do all of the work for the first
3 or 4 levels, and then further, all the way to mine town/sokoban.  You will
find this strategy difficult at first, but once you get the hang of it
you'll find yourself living a lot longer.

